#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186719310 :question/problem "![Curve and its measurements](images/3b/3be3d460f2800fcc90d02d617ee7c446b17c6fe5f25800081b7cd4f5ea9a3b36.png)\n\nCalculate\n\n$$\n\\int_C\\,\\mathrm{d}s,\n$$\n\nwhere $C$ is the semicircular arc with radius $8$ shown, traversed once counterclockwise. The dashed guide is not part of the path." :question/worked-solution "The line integral of unity with respect to arc length equals the length $L$ of the path:\n\n$$\n\\int_C\\,\\mathrm{d}s=L.\n$$\n\nThe supplied radius determines the diameter:\n\n$$\nd=2r=2(8)=16.\n$$\n\nThe path is half of a circle. Using circumference from diameter,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L&=\\frac{\\pi d}{2}\\\\&=\\frac{\\pi(16)}{2}=8 \\pi.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\int_C\\,\\mathrm{d}s=8 \\pi.\n$$\n\nReversing the direction leaves traveled length unchanged."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186719311 :question/problem "![Curve and its measurements](images/d2/d2a26cc541e294c54c14b2495ebc0a14bbd820721a510cd22c5bdb6e3a670559.png)\n\nCalculate\n\n$$\n\\int_C\\,\\mathrm{d}s,\n$$\n\nwhere $C$ is the line segment shown, traversed once from $B$ to $A$." :question/worked-solution "The line integral of unity with respect to arc length equals the length $L$ of the path:\n\n$$\n\\int_C\\,\\mathrm{d}s=L.\n$$\n\nThe endpoints are $A(1,2)$ and $B(6,8)$. The dotted right triangle is an auxiliary construction, not additional path.\n\n![Auxiliary right triangle](images/de/de466b36bcd7e3e39c77ba45055dba3d9fed3846d40940184e65a6fb8508d8bf.png)\n\nUse the two coordinate differences:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}d=AB&=\\sqrt{(6-(1))^2+(8-(2))^2}\\\\&=\\sqrt{25+36}=\\sqrt{61}=\\sqrt{61}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHere the path is the segment itself, so\n\n$$\nL=d=\\sqrt{61}.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\int_C\\,\\mathrm{d}s=\\sqrt{61}.\n$$\n\nReversing the direction leaves traveled length unchanged."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186719344 :question/problem "![Triangle ABC with the given side lengths and included angle](images/d4/d44a2489624bbf83404fd0d663e57d9cf60fe107148ce3672c8e0f85fdd91bb4.png)\n\nThe area of $\\triangle ABC$ is $12\\,\\text{cm}^2$. Find $AC$.\n\nUse $\\mathcal A=\\frac12(AB)(AC)\\sin A$ and $\\sin 30^\\circ=\\frac12$.\n" :question/worked-solution "The angle at $A$ is between $AB$ and $AC$. Let $x$ be the length of $AC$ in centimeters. Substitute the area, $AB=6$, and the supplied sine value:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n12 &= \\frac12\\cdot 6\\cdot x\\cdot\\frac12 \\\\\n12 &= \\frac32 x \\\\\nx &= 12\\cdot\\frac23=8.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $AC=8\\,\\text{cm}$. Checking gives $\\frac12\\cdot6\\cdot8\\cdot\\frac12=12\\,\\text{cm}^2$.\n"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186719349 :question/problem "![Triangle ABC with the given side lengths and included angle](images/af/af267754b67b2b9485f81406d3b104941c2b543f885a4a39729a8dfe12dcbdf6.png)\n\nThe area of $\\triangle ABC$ is $12\\,\\text{cm}^2$. The angle $A$ is obtuse. Find its exact measure.\n\n$m\\angle A=$ {{field-1}} $^\\circ$.\n" :question/worked-solution "The known sides $AB=4\\sqrt2\\,\\text{cm}$ and $AC=6\\,\\text{cm}$ meet at $A$. Apply the area formula and isolate the sine:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\mathcal A &= \\frac12(AB)(AC)\\sin A \\\\\n12 &= \\frac12\\cdot4\\sqrt2\\cdot6\\sin A \\\\\n12 &= 12\\sqrt2\\sin A \\\\\n\\sin A &= \\frac1{\\sqrt2}=\\frac{\\sqrt2}{2}.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWithin a triangle, both $45^\\circ$ and $135^\\circ=180^\\circ-45^\\circ$ have this sine. Since $A$ is obtuse, choose $135^\\circ$.\n\nTherefore, $m\\angle A=135^\\circ$. Checking the area gives $\\frac12\\cdot4\\sqrt2\\cdot6\\cdot\\frac{\\sqrt2}{2}=12\\,\\text{cm}^2$.\n"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186719353 :question/problem "![Triangle ABC with the given side lengths and included angle](images/a9/a93948e32049d3dd2554108aa8be39aee76ee34cd754c41d7967d86c05040af2.png)\n\nThe area of $\\triangle ABC$ is $15\\sqrt{3}\\,\\text{cm}^2$. Find $AC$.\n\n$AC=$ {{field-1}} $\\text{cm}$.\n" :question/worked-solution "The $60^\\circ$ angle is included between $AB=10\\,\\text{cm}$ and the unknown side $AC$. Let $x$ be the length of $AC$ in centimeters. Use $\\mathcal A=\\frac12(AB)(AC)\\sin A$ and recall that $\\sin 60^\\circ=\\frac{\\sqrt3}{2}$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n15\\sqrt3 &= \\frac12\\cdot10\\cdot x\\cdot\\frac{\\sqrt3}{2} \\\\\n15\\sqrt3 &= \\frac{5\\sqrt3}{2}x \\\\\n15 &= \\frac52 x \\\\\nx &= 6.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus $AC=6\\,\\text{cm}$. Substitution gives $\\frac12\\cdot10\\cdot6\\cdot\\frac{\\sqrt3}{2}=15\\sqrt3\\,\\text{cm}^2$.\n"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186719416 :question/problem "The dashed diagonal $BD$ divides parallelogram $ABCD$ into two congruent triangles. Find the area of the parallelogram, rounded to the nearest tenth.\n\n![](images/ea/ea69a710d57a04c208486f77f7d979a97910ce347090076f449c8c15896d2076.png)\n\nFor a triangle, use $A=\\frac12 ab\\sin C$, where $C$ is the angle between sides $a$ and $b$. Find the area of $\\triangle ABD$, then double it.\n\nArea: {{field-1}} square units.\n\nEnter the rounded number using digits and a decimal point. A scientific calculator in degree mode may be used." :question/worked-solution "The sides $AB=9$ and $AD=7$ enclose the $50^\\circ$ angle in $\\triangle ABD$.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nA_{ABD}&=\\frac12(9)(7)\\sin50^\\circ\\\\\n&=31.5\\sin50^\\circ\\\\\n&\\approx24.1304.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe two triangles are congruent, so the parallelogram has twice this area:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nA_{ABCD}&=2A_{ABD}\\\\\n&=63\\sin50^\\circ\\\\\n&\\approx48.2608.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nKeeping full calculator precision until the final rounding gives $\\boxed{48.3}$ square units."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186719418 :question/problem "Find the area of quadrilateral $ABCD$, rounded to the nearest tenth. All side lengths are in units.\n\n![](images/37/37a061b85d2c848277c6307356d0f0c8411060146c898d3f906037a381c2b9a1.png)\n\nA scientific calculator in degree mode may be used." :question/worked-solution "Draw diagonal $BD$. It splits the quadrilateral into $\\triangle ABD$ and $\\triangle BCD$, whose areas must be added.\n\n![](images/cc/cc69a1e3b37e7d77be2b39122dbf17c20daa86a8fe7c6d8578f9fd55dcb83f90.png)\n\nIn $\\triangle ABD$, the $60^\\circ$ angle is between the sides of lengths $8$ and $12$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nA_{ABD}&=\\frac12(8)(12)\\sin60^\\circ\\\\\n&=48\\sin60^\\circ\\\\\n&\\approx41.5692.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn $\\triangle BCD$, the $120^\\circ$ angle is between the sides of lengths $4$ and $8$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nA_{BCD}&=\\frac12(4)(8)\\sin120^\\circ\\\\\n&=16\\sin120^\\circ\\\\\n&\\approx13.8564.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAdd the areas, retaining full calculator precision:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nA_{ABCD}&=A_{ABD}+A_{BCD}\\\\\n&=48\\sin60^\\circ+16\\sin120^\\circ\\\\\n&\\approx55.4256.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo the nearest tenth, the area is $\\boxed{55.4}$ square units. Doubling either triangle's area would be incorrect: these two triangles are not congruent."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186719421 :question/problem "In convex quadrilateral $ABCD$, $AB=10$, $AD=8$, and the diagonal $AC=12$. The full angle $\\angle DAB=110^\\circ$ and $\\angle BAC=35^\\circ$.\n\n![](images/05/05dfe4b721ff22ac11aa4e3319d0cb4caa144cb209a4f812aa30c6b6e034f8a3.png)\n\nFind the area of $ABCD$, rounded to the nearest tenth.\n\nArea: {{field-1}} square units.\n\nEnter the rounded number using digits and a decimal point. A scientific calculator in degree mode may be used." :question/worked-solution "Diagonal $AC$ lies inside the convex quadrilateral and splits the full angle at $A$. Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\angle CAD=110^\\circ-35^\\circ=75^\\circ.\n$$\n\n![](images/7c/7c7c4df1668bad7dde4f65e0ed7af6f54fc6f5c0d435bf6f3539c274d8321f66.png)\n\nUse the shared side $AC=12$ in both triangles. The included angle for the sides $AB$ and $AC$ is $35^\\circ$, whereas the included angle for $AC$ and $AD$ is $75^\\circ$.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nA_{ABC}&=\\frac12(10)(12)\\sin35^\\circ\\\\\n&=60\\sin35^\\circ\\\\\n&\\approx34.4146,\\\\[4pt]\nA_{ACD}&=\\frac12(12)(8)\\sin75^\\circ\\\\\n&=48\\sin75^\\circ\\\\\n&\\approx46.3644.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe triangles occupy the whole quadrilateral without overlapping, so add their areas:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nA_{ABCD}&=A_{ABC}+A_{ACD}\\\\\n&=60\\sin35^\\circ+48\\sin75^\\circ\\\\\n&\\approx80.7790.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nRound only the final total: $\\boxed{80.8}$ square units. The full $110^\\circ$ angle is not an included angle of either triangle in this decomposition."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186719513 :question/problem "![Concave quadrilateral ABCD with dashed segment BD](images/0a/0a75121a020299d669f3dff640880bd837dd270911c60e955063b04b85e57018.png)\n\nThe area of the quadrilateral $ABCD$ is $28\\,\\text{cm}^{2}.$ The enclosing triangle $ABD$ has area $40\\,\\text{cm}^{2}$. What is the area of $\\triangle BCD$?" :question/worked-solution "The dashed segment $BD$ completes the enclosing triangle $ABD$. Its area is the sum of the areas of the quadrilateral and the missing triangle:\n\n$$\nA_{ABD}=A_{ABCD}+A_{BCD}.\n$$\n\nSubtract the quadrilateral's area:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nA_{BCD}&=A_{ABD}-A_{ABCD}\\\\\n&=40-28\\\\\n&=12\\,\\text{cm}^{2}.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186719516 :question/problem "![Concave quadrilateral ABCD with dashed segment BD](images/87/872338605084c225802cf1e5b7069938fb5d6f61b9cbe85312d28ecc2baf8852.png)\n\nThe area of the quadrilateral $ABCD$ is $32\\,\\text{cm}^{2}.$ The area of $\\triangle BCD$, rounded to the **nearest integer**, is {{field-1}} $\\text{cm}^{2}$.\n\nA scientific calculator is required." :question/worked-solution "The known sides $AB=12\\,\\text{cm}$ and $AD=9\\,\\text{cm}$ meet at the included angle $112^\\circ$. Use the sine-area formula for the enclosing triangle $ABD$, with the calculator in degree mode:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nA_{ABD}&=\\frac12\\cdot AB\\cdot AD\\cdot\\sin\\angle BAD\\\\\n&=\\frac12\\cdot12\\cdot9\\cdot\\sin112^\\circ\\\\\n&=54\\sin112^\\circ\\\\\n&\\approx50.067928\\,\\text{cm}^{2}.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe triangle $BCD$ is the part of $ABD$ outside the quadrilateral. Subtract before rounding, keeping the calculator's full precision:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nA_{BCD}&=A_{ABD}-A_{ABCD}\\\\\n&=54\\sin112^\\circ-32\\\\\n&\\approx18.067928\\,\\text{cm}^{2}.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nRounded to the nearest integer, its area is $18\\,\\text{cm}^{2}$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186719520 :question/problem "![Concave quadrilateral ABCD with dashed segment BD](images/45/452345c7dbf36abe26d85eb4cfaadd88b48e5dc91b12027d491be12101e98670.png)\n\nThe area of the quadrilateral $ABCD$ is $31\\,\\text{cm}^{2}.$ The angle at $A$ is a right angle. What is the area of $\\triangle BCD$?" :question/worked-solution "First find the area of the enclosing triangle $ABD$. Since $AB$ and $AD$ are perpendicular, they are its base and height:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nA_{ABD}&=\\frac12\\cdot AB\\cdot AD\\\\\n&=\\frac12\\cdot8\\cdot12\\\\\n&=48\\,\\text{cm}^{2}.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe enclosing triangle consists of the quadrilateral and the missing triangle $BCD$, so\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nA_{BCD}&=A_{ABD}-A_{ABCD}\\\\\n&=48-31\\\\\n&=17\\,\\text{cm}^{2}.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186719563 :question/problem "Let $f(x,y)=x^2+y.$ The curve $C$ is parametrized by\n$$\n\\mathbf r(t)=\\langle 2t,t^2+1\\rangle,\\qquad 0\\leq t\\leq 1,\n$$\nwith orientation given by increasing $t.$ Evaluate\n$$\n\\int_C\\nabla f\\cdot\\mathrm d\\mathbf r.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "The potential $f$ has a continuous gradient everywhere, and $\\mathbf r$ is smooth. The fundamental theorem for line integrals gives\n$$\n\\int_C\\nabla f\\cdot\\mathrm d\\mathbf r=f(\\mathbf r(1))-f(\\mathbf r(0)).\n$$\nThe initial and terminal points are\n$$\n\\mathbf r(0)=\\langle 0,1\\rangle,\\qquad \\mathbf r(1)=\\langle 2,2\\rangle.\n$$\nEvaluate the potential at both endpoints, then subtract the initial value:\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\int_C\\nabla f\\cdot\\mathrm d\\mathbf r\n&=f(2,2)-f(0,1)\\\\\n&=(2^2+2)-(0^2+1)\\\\\n&=6-1\\\\\n&=5.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186719565 :question/problem "Let $f(x,y,z)=x\\sin y+y\\cos z.$ The curve $C$ is parametrized by\n$$\n\\mathbf r(t)=\\langle\\sin t,t,2t\\rangle,\\qquad \\frac{\\pi}{2}\\leq t\\leq\\pi,\n$$\nwith orientation given by increasing $t.$ Evaluate\n$$\n\\int_C\\nabla f\\cdot\\mathrm d\\mathbf r.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "The potential has a continuous gradient everywhere and the curve is smooth, so the fundamental theorem for line integrals applies:\n$$\n\\int_C\\nabla f\\cdot\\mathrm d\\mathbf r\n=f(\\mathbf r(\\pi))-f\\left(\\mathbf r\\left(\\frac{\\pi}{2}\\right)\\right).\n$$\nThe endpoints are\n$$\n\\mathbf r\\left(\\frac{\\pi}{2}\\right)=\\left\\langle 1,\\frac{\\pi}{2},\\pi\\right\\rangle,\n\\qquad \\mathbf r(\\pi)=\\langle 0,\\pi,2\\pi\\rangle.\n$$\nTheir potential values are\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf\\left(1,\\frac{\\pi}{2},\\pi\\right)&=1\\sin\\left(\\frac{\\pi}{2}\\right)+\\frac{\\pi}{2}\\cos\\pi=1-\\frac{\\pi}{2},\\\\\nf(0,\\pi,2\\pi)&=0\\sin\\pi+\\pi\\cos(2\\pi)=\\pi.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\nTherefore,\n$$\n\\int_C\\nabla f\\cdot\\mathrm d\\mathbf r\n=\\pi-\\left(1-\\frac{\\pi}{2}\\right)=\\frac{3\\pi}{2}-1.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186719574 :question/problem "Let $f(x,y,z)=xy+z$ and let $b>1.$ The curve $C$ is parametrized by\n$$\n\\mathbf r(t)=\\langle t,t,t^2+1\\rangle,\\qquad 1\\leq t\\leq b,\n$$\nwith orientation given by increasing $t.$ If\n$$\n\\int_C\\nabla f\\cdot\\mathrm d\\mathbf r=16,\n$$\nwhat is $b$?" :question/worked-solution "The potential has a continuous gradient everywhere and the curve is smooth. By the fundamental theorem for line integrals,\n$$\n16=f(\\mathbf r(b))-f(\\mathbf r(1)).\n$$\nThe initial point is $\\mathbf r(1)=\\langle 1,1,2\\rangle,$ and the terminal point is $\\mathbf r(b)=\\langle b,b,b^2+1\\rangle.$ Thus,\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf(\\mathbf r(1))&=1\\cdot 1+2=3,\\\\\nf(\\mathbf r(b))&=b\\cdot b+(b^2+1)=2b^2+1.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\nSubstitute these values into the endpoint difference and solve:\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n16&=(2b^2+1)-3\\\\\n16&=2b^2-2\\\\\nb^2&=9.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\nSince $b>1,$ the required value is $b=3.$ Substituting it back gives $f(3,3,10)-f(1,1,2)=19-3=16.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186719587 :question/problem "![](images/0b/0b5deef545caf2423c2d146a66c7668a7e78a9eb3d8fcb77426a8369adcf85d0.png)\n\nIn $\\triangle ABC$, $AC=8$ and $AB=14$. The included angle is $\\angle CAB=109^{\\circ}$.\n\nFind the area of $\\triangle ABC$, in square units. Round your answer to **two decimal places**.\n\n> Use a scientific calculator in degree mode." :question/worked-solution "The sides $AC=8$ and $AB=14$ meet at $A$, so $109^{\\circ}$ is their included angle.\n\nApply the triangle area formula to those two sides and their included angle.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\mathcal A&=\\frac12(AC)(AB)\\sin(\\angle CAB)\\\\\n&=\\frac12(8)(14)\\sin 109^{\\circ}\\\\\n&=56\\sin 109^{\\circ}\\\\\n&\\approx 52.94904023\\\\\n&\\approx 52.95.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe area is $52.95$ square units. Keep full calculator precision until the final rounding."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186719588 :question/problem "![](images/66/665061a3ef5b9857509707bb3833ad70ab7ec4f80acc828ec6706193608551a6.png)\n\nIn $\\triangle ABC$, $CB=10$ and $CA=16$. The included angle is $\\angle BCA=56^{\\circ}$.\n\nFind the area of $\\triangle ABC$, in square units. Round your answer to **two decimal places**.\n\n> Use a scientific calculator in degree mode." :question/worked-solution "The sides $CB=10$ and $CA=16$ meet at $C$, so $56^{\\circ}$ is their included angle.\n\nApply the triangle area formula to those two sides and their included angle.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\mathcal A&=\\frac12(CB)(CA)\\sin(\\angle BCA)\\\\\n&=\\frac12(10)(16)\\sin 56^{\\circ}\\\\\n&=80\\sin 56^{\\circ}\\\\\n&\\approx 66.32300580\\\\\n&\\approx 66.32.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe area is $66.32$ square units. Keep full calculator precision until the final rounding."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186719591 :question/problem "![](images/7b/7bc75c34b1669f67272f4c5f82a290d6a100704f8c2b1f4731fe12db606e6f97.png)\n\nIn $\\triangle ABC$, $CA=11$ and $CB=6$. The included angle is $\\angle ACB=104^{\\circ}$.\n\nRounded to **nearest tenth**, the area of $\\triangle ABC$ is {{field-1}} square units.\n\n> Use a scientific calculator in degree mode." :question/worked-solution "The sides $CA=11$ and $CB=6$ meet at $C$, so $104^{\\circ}$ is their included angle.\n\nApply the triangle area formula to those two sides and their included angle.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\mathcal A&=\\frac12(CA)(CB)\\sin(\\angle ACB)\\\\\n&=\\frac12(11)(6)\\sin 104^{\\circ}\\\\\n&=33\\sin 104^{\\circ}\\\\\n&\\approx 32.01975897\\\\\n&\\approx 32.0.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe area is $32.0$ square units. Keep full calculator precision until the final rounding."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186719601 :question/problem "![](images/8e/8ef57b5ff247ada9b011c42923b0148fce2302b6f6a67f5a2a4d7e5ca53248b1.png)\n\nIn $\\triangle ABC$, $CB=17$ and $CA=8$. The included angle is $\\angle BCA=95^{\\circ}$.\n\nRounded to **nearest integer**, the area of $\\triangle ABC$ is {{field-1}} square units.\n\n> Use a scientific calculator in degree mode." :question/worked-solution "The sides $CB=17$ and $CA=8$ meet at $C$, so $95^{\\circ}$ is their included angle.\n\nApply the triangle area formula to those two sides and their included angle.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\mathcal A&=\\frac12(CB)(CA)\\sin(\\angle BCA)\\\\\n&=\\frac12(17)(8)\\sin 95^{\\circ}\\\\\n&=68\\sin 95^{\\circ}\\\\\n&\\approx 67.74123947\\\\\n&\\approx 68.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe area is $68$ square units. Keep full calculator precision until the final rounding."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186719605 :question/problem "![](images/d1/d11b09adf65955d5ff02adc7b54d2960fad9156a84bf0fda34c6954a41ebc315.png)\n\nIn $\\triangle ABC$, $BC=11$ and $BA=13$. The included angle is $\\angle CBA=90^{\\circ}$.\n\nRounded to **nearest integer**, the area of $\\triangle ABC$ is {{field-1}} square units.\n\n> Use a scientific calculator in degree mode." :question/worked-solution "The sides $BC=11$ and $BA=13$ meet at $B$, so $90^{\\circ}$ is their included angle.\n\nApply the triangle area formula to those two sides and their included angle.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\mathcal A&=\\frac12(BC)(BA)\\sin(\\angle CBA)\\\\\n&=\\frac12(11)(13)\\sin 90^{\\circ}\\\\\n&=71.5\\sin 90^{\\circ}\\\\\n&\\approx 71.50000000\\\\\n&\\approx 72.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe area is $72$ square units. Keep full calculator precision until the final rounding."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186719660 :question/problem "Consider the curve $C$, traversed in the direction of increasing $t$, where\n\n$$\n\\mathbf r(t)=\\left\\langle 1+\\sin\\left(\\frac{\\pi t}{2}\\right),1+t\\right\\rangle,\\qquad 0\\le t\\le 1.\n$$\n\nEvaluate\n\n$$\n\\int_C \\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathrm d\\mathbf r\n$$\n\nfor $\\mathbf F(x,y)=(2xy+y)\\,\\mathbf i+(x^2+x-2y)\\,\\mathbf j$.\n\n*Hint: You may wish to use the fact that $\\mathbf F$ is conservative.*" :question/worked-solution "We seek $f$ with $f_x=2xy+y$ and $f_y=x^2+x-2y$. Integrating $f_x$ with respect to $x$ gives\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=x^2y+xy+g(y).\n$$\n\nNow match its $y$ derivative to the second component:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx^2+x+g'(y)&=x^2+x-2y,\\\\\ng'(y)&=-2y,\\\\\ng(y)&=-y^2+K.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nChoose $K=0$, giving $f(x,y)=x^2y+xy-y^2$. Its gradient is exactly $\\mathbf F$.\n\nThe endpoints are\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\mathbf r(0)&=\\langle 1,1\\rangle,\\\\\n\\mathbf r(1)&=\\langle 2,2\\rangle.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe field is continuous, so the fundamental theorem for line integrals gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\int_C \\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathrm d\\mathbf r\n&=f(2,2)-f(1,1)\\\\\n&=(8+4-4)-(1+1-1)\\\\\n&=8-1\\\\\n&=7.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe term $-y^2$ is essential: integrating only the first component does not determine the whole potential."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186719674 :question/problem "Consider the curve $C$, traversed in the direction of increasing $t$, where\n\n$$\n\\mathbf r(t)=\\langle 1+t,2+t\\rangle,\\qquad 0\\le t\\le 1.\n$$\n\nEvaluate\n\n$$\n\\int_C \\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathrm d\\mathbf r\n$$\n\nfor $\\mathbf F(x,y)=y\\,\\mathbf i+x\\,\\mathbf j$.\n\n*Hint: $\\mathbf F$ is conservative. Find a potential function, then subtract its value at the starting point from its value at the ending point.*" :question/worked-solution "A potential function $f$ must satisfy $f_x=y$ and $f_y=x$. Integrating the first equation with respect to $x$ gives\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=xy+g(y).\n$$\n\nDifferentiating with respect to $y$ and matching the second component gives $x+g'(y)=x$, so $g$ is constant. We can choose\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=xy.\n$$\n\nThe curve starts at $\\mathbf r(0)=\\langle 1,2\\rangle$ and ends at $\\mathbf r(1)=\\langle 2,3\\rangle$. Since $\\nabla f=\\mathbf F$ is continuous along $C$, the fundamental theorem for line integrals gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\int_C \\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathrm d\\mathbf r\n&=f(2,3)-f(1,2)\\\\\n&=2\\cdot 3-1\\cdot 2\\\\\n&=4.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe arbitrary constant in a potential cancels in this difference."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186719678 :question/problem "Consider the vector field\n\n$$\n\\mathbf F(x,y)=2xe^{-y}\\,\\mathbf i+(2y-x^2e^{-y})\\,\\mathbf j.\n$$\n\nEvaluate\n\n$$\n\\int_C \\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathrm d\\mathbf r,\n$$\n\nwhere $C$ is the curve $y=\\ln(x-1)$ for $2\\le x\\le 4$, traversed in the direction of increasing $x$." :question/worked-solution "Write $P=2xe^{-y}$ and $Q=2y-x^2e^{-y}$. These functions have continuous partial derivatives on $\\mathbb R^2$, and\n\n$$\nP_y=Q_x=-2xe^{-y}.\n$$\n\nSince $\\mathbb R^2$ is simply connected, $\\mathbf F$ is conservative. To construct a potential, integrate $f_x=P$ with respect to $x$:\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=x^2e^{-y}+g(y).\n$$\n\nMatching $f_y$ to $Q$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n-x^2e^{-y}+g'(y)&=2y-x^2e^{-y},\\\\\ng'(y)&=2y,\\\\\ng(y)&=y^2+K.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe may choose $K=0$, so $f(x,y)=x^2e^{-y}+y^2$. Differentiation verifies both components of $\\mathbf F$.\n\nA parametrization consistent with the orientation is $\\mathbf r(t)=\\langle t,\\ln(t-1)\\rangle$ for $2\\le t\\le 4$. The curve is smooth on this interval, starts at $(2,0)$, and ends at $(4,\\ln 3)$.\n\nApply the fundamental theorem for line integrals, using $e^{-\\ln 3}=1/3$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\int_C \\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathrm d\\mathbf r\n&=f(4,\\ln 3)-f(2,0)\\\\\n&=\\left(\\frac{16}{3}+(\\ln 3)^2\\right)-4\\\\\n&=\\frac{4}{3}+(\\ln 3)^2.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nOnly the oriented endpoints enter this final evaluation; differentiating the logarithmic path and integrating the resulting dot product is unnecessary."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186719803 :question/problem "Find the area of convex quadrilateral $ABCD$, rounded to the **nearest tenth**. All lengths are in units.\n\n$DA=10$, $DC=9$, $DB=13$, $\\angle ADB=70^\\circ$, $\\angle BDC=25^\\circ$.\n\n![](images/20/2078f6e3479cfe8e9d176fb6008c4faa2c2a60dcb1fe3c800e7a002cdb9246a3.png)\n\nArea: {{field-1}} square units. Enter the rounded number using digits and a decimal point.\n\nA scientific calculator in degree mode may be used." :question/worked-solution "Diagonal $DB$ divides the quadrilateral into two nonoverlapping triangles.\n\n![](images/20/2078f6e3479cfe8e9d176fb6008c4faa2c2a60dcb1fe3c800e7a002cdb9246a3.png)\n\nIn $\\triangle DAB$, the sides of lengths $10$ and $13$ enclose the $70^\\circ$ angle. Applying the triangle area formula gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nA_{DAB}&=\\frac12(10)(13)\\sin 70^\\circ\\\\\n&=65\\sin 70^\\circ\\\\\n&\\approx 61.0800.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn $\\triangle DBC$, the sides of lengths $13$ and $9$ enclose the $25^\\circ$ angle. Applying the triangle area formula gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nA_{DBC}&=\\frac12(13)(9)\\sin 25^\\circ\\\\\n&=58.5\\sin 25^\\circ\\\\\n&\\approx 24.7232.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAdd the two areas, retaining full calculator precision:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nA_{ABCD}&=A_{DAB}+A_{DBC}\\\\\n&=65\\sin 70^\\circ+58.5\\sin 25^\\circ\\\\\n&\\approx 85.8032.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nRound only the final total: $\\boxed{85.8}$ square units."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186719804 :question/problem "Find the area of convex quadrilateral $ABCD$, rounded to the **nearest tenth**. All lengths are in units.\n\n$AB=9$, $AD=6$, $\\angle BAD=120^\\circ$, $BC=9$, $CD=15$, $\\angle BCD=60^\\circ$.\n\n![](images/e1/e1a0b34b59d2f6a3fe0242203cc5a83cdc620c8c3ddeec76a1e5bf5ce6b1412c.png)\n\nArea: {{field-1}} square units. Enter the rounded number using digits and a decimal point.\n\nA scientific calculator in degree mode may be used." :question/worked-solution "Diagonal $BD$ divides the quadrilateral into two nonoverlapping triangles.\n\n![](images/00/0064faca873ce4706f5b4cd612bb4b8f26ad0527229370fb08d41d1d9ef1f121.png)\n\nIn $\\triangle ABD$, the sides of lengths $9$ and $6$ enclose the $120^\\circ$ angle. Applying the triangle area formula gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nA_{ABD}&=\\frac12(9)(6)\\sin 120^\\circ\\\\\n&=27\\sin 120^\\circ\\\\\n&\\approx 23.3827.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn $\\triangle BCD$, the sides of lengths $9$ and $15$ enclose the $60^\\circ$ angle. Applying the triangle area formula gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nA_{BCD}&=\\frac12(9)(15)\\sin 60^\\circ\\\\\n&=67.5\\sin 60^\\circ\\\\\n&\\approx 58.4567.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAdd the two areas, retaining full calculator precision:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nA_{ABCD}&=A_{ABD}+A_{BCD}\\\\\n&=27\\sin 120^\\circ+67.5\\sin 60^\\circ\\\\\n&\\approx 81.8394.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nRound only the final total: $\\boxed{81.8}$ square units."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186719810 :question/problem "Find the area of parallelogram $ABCD$, rounded to the **nearest tenth**. All lengths are in units.\n\n$AB=13$, $AD=9$, $\\angle BAD=65^\\circ$.\n\n![](images/bb/bba7e1b0a80df6b27d62bb34fd49e3cc5237c91eecc80adfacefd6390da8064c.png)\n\nArea: {{field-1}} square units. Enter the rounded number using digits and a decimal point.\n\nA scientific calculator in degree mode may be used." :question/worked-solution "Diagonal $BD$ divides the parallelogram into two congruent triangles.\n\n![](images/fb/fb8c290577729f96ae47a549413b77aa6bfd4d4df85d067a51e90a4aceff21d7.png)\n\nIn $\\triangle ABD$, the sides of lengths $13$ and $9$ enclose the $65^\\circ$ angle. Applying the triangle area formula gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nA_{ABD}&=\\frac12(13)(9)\\sin 65^\\circ\\\\\n&=58.5\\sin 65^\\circ\\\\\n&\\approx 53.0190.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nDouble the triangle area, retaining full calculator precision:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nA_{ABCD}&=2A_{ABD}\\\\\n&=2\\left(58.5\\sin 65^\\circ\\right)\\\\\n&\\approx 106.0380.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nRound only the final total: $\\boxed{106.0}$ square units."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186719816 :question/problem "Find the area of convex quadrilateral $ABCD$, rounded to the **nearest tenth**. All lengths are in units.\n\n$AB=6$, $AD=8$, $\\angle BAD=90^\\circ$, $BC=10$, $CD=10$, $\\angle BCD=60^\\circ$.\n\n![](images/ea/ea16d722bca35c459261b92c804c645064a95f33d7093ef989427e6a96a7d78b.png)\n\nA scientific calculator in degree mode may be used." :question/worked-solution "Diagonal $BD$ divides the quadrilateral into two nonoverlapping triangles.\n\n![](images/c5/c5fd00ab665309b62eee056be4805a0e9d035cdec734f3cd2789c21e8bec4ead.png)\n\nIn $\\triangle ABD$, the sides of lengths $6$ and $8$ enclose the $90^\\circ$ angle. Applying the triangle area formula gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nA_{ABD}&=\\frac12(6)(8)\\sin 90^\\circ\\\\\n&=24\\sin 90^\\circ\\\\\n&\\approx 24.0000.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn $\\triangle BCD$, the sides of lengths $10$ and $10$ enclose the $60^\\circ$ angle. Applying the triangle area formula gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nA_{BCD}&=\\frac12(10)(10)\\sin 60^\\circ\\\\\n&=50\\sin 60^\\circ\\\\\n&\\approx 43.3013.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAdd the two areas, retaining full calculator precision:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nA_{ABCD}&=A_{ABD}+A_{BCD}\\\\\n&=24\\sin 90^\\circ+50\\sin 60^\\circ\\\\\n&\\approx 67.3013.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nRound only the final total: $\\boxed{67.3}$ square units."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186719819 :question/problem "Find the area of parallelogram $ABCD$, rounded to the **nearest tenth**. All lengths are in units.\n\n$BC=10$, $BA=8$, $\\angle CBA=70^\\circ$.\n\n![](images/05/05ebb7c12319da9a8cc5424eacc1dd4a993a878af92a82d244c37ec137b87de8.png)\n\nA scientific calculator in degree mode may be used." :question/worked-solution "Diagonal $CA$ divides the parallelogram into two congruent triangles.\n\n![](images/7c/7c049d5968e889022d5c626558ec90aee4140d7ddd987a3a10b679f0cde62d31.png)\n\nIn $\\triangle BCA$, the sides of lengths $10$ and $8$ enclose the $70^\\circ$ angle. Applying the triangle area formula gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nA_{BCA}&=\\frac12(10)(8)\\sin 70^\\circ\\\\\n&=40\\sin 70^\\circ\\\\\n&\\approx 37.5877.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nDouble the triangle area, retaining full calculator precision:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nA_{ABCD}&=2A_{BCA}\\\\\n&=2\\left(40\\sin 70^\\circ\\right)\\\\\n&\\approx 75.1754.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nRound only the final total: $\\boxed{75.2}$ square units."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186719834 :question/problem "![Triangle CAB with its given side lengths and included angle](images/0b/0b2c72bd9c35ad12f10c9a7ca58d3a03a4fe62f4d2d75cd2871c4f1cf6f04757.png)\n\nThe area of $\\triangle CAB$ is $8 \\sqrt{2}\\,\\text{cm}^2$. Find $CB$." :question/worked-solution "The angle at $C$ is included between $CA$ and $CB$. Let $x$ be the length of $CB$ in cm. Use $\\sin 45^\\circ=\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\mathcal A&=\\frac12(CA)(CB)\\sin C \\\\\n8 \\sqrt{2}&=\\frac12\\cdot\\left(8\\right)\\cdot x\\cdot\\sin 45^\\circ \\\\\n8 \\sqrt{2}&=\\frac12\\cdot\\left(8\\right)\\cdot x\\cdot\\left(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\right) \\\\\n8 \\sqrt{2}&=\\left(2 \\sqrt{2}\\right)x \\\\\nx&=\\frac{8 \\sqrt{2}}{2 \\sqrt{2}}=4\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus $CB=4\\,\\text{cm}$. Checking the area gives $\\frac12\\cdot\\left(8\\right)\\cdot\\left(4\\right)\\cdot\\left(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\right)=8 \\sqrt{2}\\,\\text{cm}^2$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186719839 :question/problem "![Triangle CAB with its given side lengths and included angle](images/40/40979bcfce5e4f446e7ce15733f5b8a3c9d35b1fe490b2342ad6bc87691db40b.png)\n\nThe area of $\\triangle CAB$ is $14 \\sqrt{3}\\,\\text{cm}^2$. Find $CB$." :question/worked-solution "The angle at $C$ is included between $CA$ and $CB$. Let $x$ be the length of $CB$ in cm. Use $\\sin 60^\\circ=\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\mathcal A&=\\frac12(CA)(CB)\\sin C \\\\\n14 \\sqrt{3}&=\\frac12\\cdot\\left(8\\right)\\cdot x\\cdot\\sin 60^\\circ \\\\\n14 \\sqrt{3}&=\\frac12\\cdot\\left(8\\right)\\cdot x\\cdot\\left(\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}\\right) \\\\\n14 \\sqrt{3}&=\\left(2 \\sqrt{3}\\right)x \\\\\nx&=\\frac{14 \\sqrt{3}}{2 \\sqrt{3}}=7\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus $CB=7\\,\\text{cm}$. Checking the area gives $\\frac12\\cdot\\left(8\\right)\\cdot\\left(7\\right)\\cdot\\left(\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}\\right)=14 \\sqrt{3}\\,\\text{cm}^2$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186719840 :question/problem "![Triangle BCA with its given side lengths and included angle](images/d7/d78faa847c9ac7c181821f5ba6fc69c0e19b2fbc3844602dac07db77d9869a2a.png)\n\nThe area of $\\triangle BCA$ is $7\\,\\text{cm}^2$. Find $BA$." :question/worked-solution "The angle at $B$ is included between $BC$ and $BA$. Let $x$ be the length of $BA$ in cm. Use $\\sin 150^\\circ=\\sin(180^\\circ-150^\\circ)=\\sin 30^\\circ=\\frac{1}{2}$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\mathcal A&=\\frac12(BC)(BA)\\sin B \\\\\n7&=\\frac12\\cdot\\left(7\\right)\\cdot x\\cdot\\sin 150^\\circ \\\\\n7&=\\frac12\\cdot\\left(7\\right)\\cdot x\\cdot\\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right) \\\\\n7&=\\left(\\frac{7}{4}\\right)x \\\\\nx&=\\frac{7}{\\frac{7}{4}}=4\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus $BA=4\\,\\text{cm}$. Checking the area gives $\\frac12\\cdot\\left(7\\right)\\cdot\\left(4\\right)\\cdot\\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right)=7\\,\\text{cm}^2$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186719847 :question/problem "![Triangle ABC with its given side lengths and included angle](images/0d/0d747478e090f092451da1828db98c6213e82187e8d48c418db0264d17bac5b4.png)\n\nThe area of $\\triangle ABC$ is $\\frac{33 \\sqrt{2}}{2}\\,\\text{mm}^2$. Find $AB$.\n\n$AB=$ {{field-1}} $\\text{mm}$." :question/worked-solution "The angle at $A$ is included between $AB$ and $AC$. Let $x$ be the length of $AB$ in mm. Use $\\sin 135^\\circ=\\sin(180^\\circ-135^\\circ)=\\sin 45^\\circ=\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\mathcal A&=\\frac12(AB)(AC)\\sin A \\\\\n\\frac{33 \\sqrt{2}}{2}&=\\frac12\\cdot\\left(6\\right)\\cdot x\\cdot\\sin 135^\\circ \\\\\n\\frac{33 \\sqrt{2}}{2}&=\\frac12\\cdot\\left(6\\right)\\cdot x\\cdot\\left(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\right) \\\\\n\\frac{33 \\sqrt{2}}{2}&=\\left(\\frac{3 \\sqrt{2}}{2}\\right)x \\\\\nx&=\\frac{\\frac{33 \\sqrt{2}}{2}}{\\frac{3 \\sqrt{2}}{2}}=11\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus $AB=11\\,\\text{mm}$. Checking the area gives $\\frac12\\cdot\\left(11\\right)\\cdot\\left(6\\right)\\cdot\\left(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\right)=\\frac{33 \\sqrt{2}}{2}\\,\\text{mm}^2$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186719853 :question/problem "![Triangle BCA with its given side lengths and included angle](images/44/4445e6e3a74dd289305ee06461147ffe8efb1bc11e4b38e7d9c580303ac43006.png)\n\nThe area of $\\triangle BCA$ is $8\\,\\text{cm}^2$. Find $BC$.\n\n$BC=$ {{field-1}} $\\text{cm}$." :question/worked-solution "The angle at $B$ is included between $BC$ and $BA$. Let $x$ be the length of $BC$ in cm. Use $\\sin 30^\\circ=\\frac{1}{2}$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\mathcal A&=\\frac12(BC)(BA)\\sin B \\\\\n8&=\\frac12\\cdot\\left(8\\right)\\cdot x\\cdot\\sin 30^\\circ \\\\\n8&=\\frac12\\cdot\\left(8\\right)\\cdot x\\cdot\\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right) \\\\\n8&=\\left(2\\right)x \\\\\nx&=\\frac{8}{2}=4\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus $BC=4\\,\\text{cm}$. Checking the area gives $\\frac12\\cdot\\left(4\\right)\\cdot\\left(8\\right)\\cdot\\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right)=8\\,\\text{cm}^2$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186719855 :question/problem "Let $f(x,y)=xy+y^2.$ Let $C$ be the part of the curve $y^2=x+1$ lying below the $x$-axis, traversed from its intersection with $x=3$ to its intersection with $x=0.$ Evaluate\n\n$$\n\\int_C \\nabla f\\cdot\\mathrm{d}\\mathbf r.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "The path lies below the $x$-axis, so $y$ must be negative. At $x=3,$ the curve equation gives $y^2=4,$ hence $y=-2.$ At $x=0,$ it gives $y^2=1,$ hence $y=-1.$ In the stated direction, the initial and terminal points are therefore $A=(3,-2)$ and $B=(0,-1).$\n\nTo describe this directed arc, set $y=t$ and write $x=t^2-1.$ Then\n\n$$\n\\mathbf r(t)=\\langle t^2-1,t\\rangle,\\qquad -2\\le t\\le-1.\n$$\n\nAs $t$ increases, $x$ decreases from $3$ to $0,$ agreeing with the prescribed traversal. The gradient is continuous everywhere. Thus,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\int_C \\nabla f\\cdot\\mathrm{d}\\mathbf r\n&=f(B)-f(A)\\\\\n&=f(0,-1)-f(3,-2)\\\\\n&=\\bigl(0\\cdot(-1)+(-1)^2\\bigr)-\\bigl(3\\cdot(-2)+(-2)^2\\bigr)\\\\\n&=1-(-2)\\\\\n&=3.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the positive values of $y$ would select a different arc. Reversing the order of the two endpoint values would change the sign of the integral."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186719869 :question/problem "Let $f(x,y)=x^2+y^2.$ Let $C$ follow the curve $\\dfrac{x}{2}=\\sqrt{y}$ as $y$ increases from $1$ to $4.$ Evaluate\n\n$$\n\\int_C \\nabla f\\cdot\\mathrm{d}\\mathbf r.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "The interval is specified in terms of $y.$ Set $y=t$ and rearrange the curve equation to obtain $x=2\\sqrt{t}.$ A suitable parametrization is\n\n$$\n\\mathbf r(t)=\\langle 2\\sqrt{t},t\\rangle,\\qquad 1\\le t\\le4.\n$$\n\nThus the initial point is $A=\\mathbf r(1)=(2,1)$ and the terminal point is $B=\\mathbf r(4)=(4,4).$ The gradient is continuous everywhere. Applying the fundamental theorem for line integrals,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\int_C \\nabla f\\cdot\\mathrm{d}\\mathbf r\n&=f(B)-f(A)\\\\\n&=f(4,4)-f(2,1)\\\\\n&=(4^2+4^2)-(2^2+1^2)\\\\\n&=32-5\\\\\n&=27.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186719875 :question/problem "Let $f(x,y)=xy.$ Let $C$ follow the curve $y=x+1$ as $x$ increases from $0$ to $2.$ Evaluate\n\n$$\n\\int_C \\nabla f\\cdot \\mathrm{d}\\mathbf r.\n$$\n\n*Hint: Use $\\mathbf r(t)=\\langle t,t+1\\rangle$ for $0\\le t\\le 2.$*" :question/worked-solution "The supplied parametrization gives the initial point $A=\\mathbf r(0)=(0,1)$ and terminal point $B=\\mathbf r(2)=(2,3).$ Since $f$ is differentiable and $\\nabla f$ is continuous everywhere, the fundamental theorem for line integrals gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\int_C \\nabla f\\cdot\\mathrm{d}\\mathbf r\n&=f(B)-f(A)\\\\\n&=f(2,3)-f(0,1)\\\\\n&=2\\cdot3-0\\cdot1\\\\\n&=6.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186719885 :question/problem "Evaluate the scalar line integral\n\n$$\n\\int_C (x^2+y)\\,\\mathrm{d}s,\n$$\n\nwhere $C$ is given by $y=2x+1$ for $0\\le x\\le 1$." :question/worked-solution "Set $x=t$ and substitute the line equation:\n\n$$\n\\mathbf r(t)=\\langle t,2t+1\\rangle,\\qquad 0\\le t\\le 1.\n$$\n\nThen\n\n$$\n\\mathbf r'(t)=\\langle 1,2\\rangle,\\qquad \\|\\mathbf r'(t)\\|=\\sqrt{1+4}=\\sqrt5.\n$$\n\nSubstitute both coordinates into the scalar function:\n\n$$\nf(\\mathbf r(t))=t^2+(2t+1)=t^2+2t+1.\n$$\n\nMultiply by the speed and integrate each term:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\int_C (x^2+y)\\,\\mathrm{d}s\n&=\\int_0^1 f(\\mathbf r(t))\\|\\mathbf r'(t)\\|\\,\\mathrm{d}t\\\\\n&=\\sqrt5\\int_0^1(t^2+2t+1)\\,\\mathrm{d}t\\\\\n&=\\sqrt5\\left[\\frac{t^3}{3}+t^2+t\\right]_0^1\\\\\n&=\\sqrt5\\left(\\frac13+1+1\\right)\\\\\n&=\\frac{7\\sqrt5}{3}.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186719895 :question/problem "Evaluate the scalar line integral\n\n$$\n\\int_C x\\,\\mathrm{d}s,\n$$\n\nwhere $C$ is given by $y=2x$ for $0\\le x\\le 1$." :question/worked-solution "Set $x=t$. The explicit equation gives\n\n$$\n\\mathbf r(t)=\\langle t,2t\\rangle,\\qquad 0\\le t\\le 1.\n$$\n\nDifferentiate the curve and compute its speed:\n\n$$\n\\mathbf r'(t)=\\langle 1,2\\rangle,\\qquad \\|\\mathbf r'(t)\\|=\\sqrt{1^2+2^2}=\\sqrt5.\n$$\n\nThe scalar function is $f(x,y)=x$, so $f(\\mathbf r(t))=t$. Include the speed factor when replacing $\\mathrm{d}s$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\int_C x\\,\\mathrm{d}s\n&=\\int_0^1 f(\\mathbf r(t))\\|\\mathbf r'(t)\\|\\,\\mathrm{d}t\\\\\n&=\\sqrt5\\int_0^1 t\\,\\mathrm{d}t\\\\\n&=\\sqrt5\\left[\\frac{t^2}{2}\\right]_0^1\\\\\n&=\\frac{\\sqrt5}{2}.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186719899 :question/problem "Evaluate the scalar line integral\n\n$$\n\\int_C 2y^2\\,\\mathrm{d}s,\n$$\n\nwhere $C$ is given by $y=e^x$ for $0\\le x\\le\\ln 2$." :question/worked-solution "Use the explicit curve to set\n\n$$\n\\mathbf r(t)=\\langle t,e^t\\rangle,\\qquad 0\\le t\\le\\ln2.\n$$\n\nThe speed is now variable:\n\n$$\n\\mathbf r'(t)=\\langle1,e^t\\rangle,\\qquad \\|\\mathbf r'(t)\\|=\\sqrt{1+e^{2t}}.\n$$\n\nSince $f(\\mathbf r(t))=2e^{2t}$, the line integral becomes\n\n$$\n\\int_C 2y^2\\,\\mathrm{d}s=\\int_0^{\\ln2}2e^{2t}\\sqrt{1+e^{2t}}\\,\\mathrm{d}t.\n$$\n\nChoose $u=1+e^{2t}$, so $\\mathrm{d}u=2e^{2t}\\,\\mathrm{d}t$. The bounds change from $t=0$ and $t=\\ln2$ to $u=2$ and $u=5$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\int_C 2y^2\\,\\mathrm{d}s\n&=\\int_2^5 u^{1/2}\\,\\mathrm{d}u\\\\\n&=\\left[\\frac23u^{3/2}\\right]_2^5\\\\\n&=\\frac23(5\\sqrt5-2\\sqrt2)\\\\\n&=\\frac{10\\sqrt5-4\\sqrt2}{3}.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186719905 :question/problem "Let $\\mathbf{a}=\\langle1,-2,1\\rangle$, $\\mathbf{b}=\\langle2,1,-1\\rangle$, and $\\mathbf{c}=\\langle-1,2,3\\rangle$. Find $\\mathbf{c}\\cdot(\\mathbf{b}\\times\\mathbf{a})$ using the definition of the scalar triple product.\n\n$\\mathbf{c}\\cdot(\\mathbf{b}\\times\\mathbf{a})=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "The requested cross product is $\\mathbf{b}\\times\\mathbf{a}$, and the vector outside the dot product is $\\mathbf{c}$.\n\nFirst, compute the cross product in the requested order:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\mathbf{b}\\times\\mathbf{a}\n&=\\begin{vmatrix}\\mathbf{i}&\\mathbf{j}&\\mathbf{k}\\\\2&1&-1\\\\1&-2&1\\end{vmatrix}\\\\\n&=\\begin{vmatrix}1&-1\\\\-2&1\\end{vmatrix}\\mathbf{i}\n-\\begin{vmatrix}2&-1\\\\1&1\\end{vmatrix}\\mathbf{j}\n+\\begin{vmatrix}2&1\\\\1&-2\\end{vmatrix}\\mathbf{k}\\\\\n&=(1(1)-(-1)(-2))\\mathbf{i}\\\\\n&\\quad -(2(1)-(-1)(1))\\mathbf{j}\\\\\n&\\quad +(2(-2)-1(1))\\mathbf{k}\\\\\n&=-\\mathbf{i}-3\\mathbf{j}-5\\mathbf{k}\\\\\n&=\\langle-1,-3,-5\\rangle.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen take the dot product with $\\mathbf{c}$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\mathbf{c}\\cdot(\\mathbf{b}\\times\\mathbf{a})\n&=\\langle-1,2,3\\rangle\\cdot\\langle-1,-3,-5\\rangle\\\\\n&=(-1)(-1)+2(-3)+3(-5)\\\\\n&=1-6-15\\\\\n&=-20.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186719906 :question/problem "Find $\\mathbf{a}\\cdot(\\mathbf{b}\\times\\mathbf{c})$ given that $\\mathbf{a}=\\langle-2,0,3\\rangle$ and $\\mathbf{b}\\times\\mathbf{c}=\\langle1,-3,2\\rangle$." :question/worked-solution "The cross product is supplied, so take its dot product with $\\mathbf{a}$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\mathbf{a}\\cdot(\\mathbf{b}\\times\\mathbf{c})\n&=\\langle-2,0,3\\rangle\\cdot\\langle1,-3,2\\rangle\\\\\n&=(-2)(1)+0(-3)+3(2)\\\\\n&=-2+0+6\\\\\n&=4.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}]}
